config ODI_NIC
	bool "odi-oss open CPU-port NIC driver (RTL9602C)"
	depends on MACH_RTL8686
	help
	  Open, GPL driver for the RTL9602C CPU-port Ethernet NIC, our own
	  implementation, written from register behaviour observed on the
	  device. No proprietary NIC driver is ever built
	  on this tree (MACH_RTL8686, the 6.18 port's own board symbol,
	  never carries one) -- selecting this builds
	  drivers/net/ethernet/odi/odi_nic.c standalone.

config ODI_OMCI
	bool "odi-oss open OMCI frame transport (pkt_redirect replacement)"
	depends on ODI_NIC
	help
	  Open, GPL netlink transport for OMCI frames between omcid and the
	  PON, our own implementation of this transport.
	  Builds drivers/net/ethernet/odi/odi_omci.c, sourcing and sinking
	  frames through the odi_nic driver's own rxhook/odi_nic_tx_words
	  exports. Depends on ODI_NIC: this transport needs the hooks only
	  the open NIC driver exports.

config ODI_SWITCH
	bool "odi-oss open switch core and OMCI command table (RTL9602C)"
	depends on ODI_OMCI
	select FW_LOADER
	select CRC32
	help
	  Open, GPL implementation of the OMCI command table (the 79-slot
	  command dispatch) and the RTL9602C switch access layer, our own
	  code. Builds drivers/net/ethernet/odi/odi_switch.c
	  (the MMIO map and register accessors), odi_switch_tbl.c (indirect
	  table primitives), odi_switch_dal.c (the per-feature leaves),
	  odi_switch_cmd.c (the command dispatch, odi_switch_cmd()) and
	  odi_reg.c (/dev/odi_sw, the register/SoC/MIB ioctls for our own
	  userland), and adds the ODI_OMCI_OP_CMD netlink op to odi_omci.c
	  that reaches it. Depends on ODI_OMCI: the netlink op rides the
	  same socket that module already owns.

	  The captured register-replay tables (module-load replay, and
	  under ODI_SDKINIT/ODI_GPON the SDK-init verbs and the GPON boot
	  init) are not built in: they are firmware files under
	  /lib/firmware/odi/, loaded with request_firmware() when a
	  trigger needs one, validated (odi_replay_blob.c: header, size,
	  CRC-32, every record) and released again. Hence FW_LOADER and
	  CRC32.

config ODI_GPON
	bool "odi-oss open GPON MAC driver (RTL9602C)"
	depends on ODI_SWITCH
	help
	  Open, GPL GPON MAC block driver: the O1-O7 activation FSM, the
	  PLOAM codec, the register leaves and the kernel-integration glue
	  (odi_gpon_fsm.o/odi_gpon_ploam.o/odi_gpon_hw.o/odi_gpon_isr.o/
	  odi_gpon_init.o/odi_gpon.o, the boot init table in
	  /lib/firmware/odi/gpon_init.bin,
	  drivers/net/ethernet/odi/) against the odi_gpon.h interface: the
	  boot-verb glue, the ISR registration, three kernel timers (TO1,
	  TO2, the BER-interval/REI send) and the /proc/odi_gpon status
	  file. Host-tested against a captured re-activation before this
	  config is ever built (make test-host, tools/regtrace/
	  gpon_replay_compare.py). Depends on ODI_SWITCH: the GPON register
	  block is reached through the same MMIO primitive odi_switch.c
	  already owns.

config ODI_SDKINIT
	bool "odi-oss SDK-init replay (rtk_init verbs and PON steps)"
	depends on ODI_GPON
	help
	  "SDK-init replay": replays the rtk_init verbs (switch svlan stp
	  oam acl qos sec rate classify stat trunk l2 vlan port mirror cpu
	  rldp trap gpio time ponmac) and PON steps (i2c i2cen gpon rxsd)
	  from a captured clean boot, register write by register write.
	  Builds drivers/net/ethernet/odi/odi_switch_sdkinit.c (the replay
	  entry point and mask); the per-verb events are
	  /lib/firmware/odi/sdkinit.bin (GENERATED by
	  tools/regtrace/mksdkinit.py), loaded for each verb.
	  The /proc/rtk_init dispatch (odi_rtk_init.c) routes each of these
	  verbs through odi_switch_sdkinit_verb(), which returns -ENOSYS and
	  logs when that verb has no data for this boot or its own bit is
	  clear in the replay mask (/var/config/sdkinit.mask). intr and irq
	  are never replayed here -- they register software interrupt
	  handlers, not switch state; ODI_INTR below covers those
	  two. Depends on ODI_GPON: the same dispatch's own CONFIG_ODI_GPON
	  block is what this guard dispatch code extends.

config ODI_INTR
	bool "odi-oss's own IRQ 8 (apl_sw) demux (RTL9602C)"
	depends on ODI_SDKINIT
	help
	  Open, GPL interrupt handling for the one shared hardware line
	  ("apl_sw", IRQ 8) this image services. Builds
	  drivers/net/ethernet/odi/odi_intr.c (the handler table, an
	  IMR/IMS-based demux, request_irq()) and turns the "intr" and
	  "irq" /proc/rtk_init verbs into no-ops, since odi_intr_init() now
	  does everything they used to (CHIP_IRQ_SETUP/IMR/IMS reset,
	  request_irq(8, ...)) unconditionally at boot instead. odi_gpon.c
	  routes its own interrupt registration/mask/clear calls to
	  odi_intr_register()/odi_intr_enable() here. Depends on
	  ODI_SDKINIT: no ordering dependency of its own, but a build with
	  our own switch-table replay and not our own interrupt demux is
	  not a configuration this codebase supports.

config ODI_BOARD
	bool "odi-oss board-init LED/I2C-core replay (RTL9602C)"
	depends on ODI_INTR
	help
	  Open, GPL board-init sequence: the LED/I2C-core register writes
	  observed at board init, before rcS ever runs. Builds
	  drivers/net/ethernet/odi/odi_board.c (the replay entry point) and
	  odi_board_data.c (the 88-event ordered register/SoC-window write
	  table, captured from a clean boot) and wires odi_board_init()
	  into the board port in their place. Once this config is on, board
	  init references no proprietary symbol at all. Depends on
	  ODI_INTR: not an ordering dependency of the replay itself
	  (odi_board_init() only needs ODI_SWITCH), but this codebase does
	  not support replaying board-init writes while still depending on
	  a different interrupt path, same reasoning as ODI_INTR depending
	  on ODI_SDKINIT above.

config ODI_SDK_OUT
	bool "odi-oss: no proprietary switch SDK linked"
	depends on ODI_BOARD
	help
	  Confirms nothing from the proprietary switch SDK is
	  linked into the kernel: by the time this is selected,
	  ODI_NIC/ODI_OMCI/ODI_SWITCH/ODI_GPON/ODI_SDKINIT/ODI_INTR are
	  already all forced on, so every entry point that SDK used to
	  serve already has a non-proprietary replacement in place:
	    - board init's core/i2c/led register writes: ODI_BOARD's own
	      replay, unchanged.
	    - odi_gpon.c's interrupt registration/mask/clear: ODI_INTR's
	      own demux, unchanged.
	    - the 26 /proc/rtk_init verbs (odi_rtk_init.c, this config
	      only): a from-scratch replacement, routing every verb to
	      odi_switch_sdkinit_verb() (the 20 switch-table steps plus
	      i2c/i2cen/gpon/rxsd) or odi_gpon_verb() (the seven GPON
	      PON-step verbs), or treating it as a no-op (intr/irq). A verb
	      this boot's replay tables have no data for returns -ENOSYS
	      and logs, rather than silently doing nothing.
	  src/diag is not guarded by this config at all: it is userland, and
	  every one of its commands reads /dev/odi_sw (odi_reg.c, ODI_SWITCH)
	  or /proc/odi_gpon directly.
	  There is no proprietary sockopt handler anywhere in this tree, and
	  no sockopt fallback in src/diag either -- it implements only the
	  16 hand-written commands its README documents, not the stock
	  `diag` binary's full command set.

config ODI_WDT
	bool "odi-oss open watchdog kicker (RTL9602C)"
	depends on ODI_SDK_OUT
	help
	  Open, GPL, from-scratch watchdog kicker kthread. Written from a
	  description of the watchdog register block (odi_wdt.h has
	  the addresses and bit layout). Builds drivers/net/ethernet/odi/odi_wdt.c:
	  the same control-register arm and kick-register kick sequence, at the
	  operating point U-Boot's own en_wdt already arms (PRESCALE=3,
	  TIMEOUT1=31, TIMEOUT2=0, RESET_MODE=0 -- the seed config's
	  CONFIG_WDT_* today), an interruptible kicker kthread at the same
	  5 s interval, and the same userland-confirmation deadline,
	  heartbeat and kicker-stall report. /proc/luna_watchdog/
	  {watchdog_flag,userland_ok} keep the same paths and contract, so
	  rootfs/skeleton/etc/init.d/rcS needs no change, and
	  wdt_pre_reset_hook (odi_nic.c, CONFIG_ODI_NIC) keeps the same
	  symbol name, so the open NIC driver quiesces its DMA engine
	  before a watchdog-forced reset.

config ODI_RAMLOG
	bool "odi-oss DRAM ring-buffer console (watchdog-reset-surviving boot log)"
	depends on MACH_RTL8686
	help
	  Open, GPL struct console driver: mirrors every console line into
	  the same two DRAM pages that survive a watchdog reset, in the
	  exact on-memory format tools/memprobe/ramlog-read.sh parses,
	  through struct console/register_console() (odi_ramlog.c).
	  odi_ramlog.h has the full format and the HEAD/JSK tradeoff this
	  driver accepts. Standalone: no dependency on the ODI_NIC..ODI_WDT
	  chain above (a console driver, not switch/GPON register work).
	  The two fixed DRAM addresses (odi_ramlog.h) are board-fact: this
	  board is 32 MB DRAM. Before its first write it saves both pages,
	  the previous boot's log, and exposes them as /proc/odi_ramlog_prev
	  (decoded) and /proc/odi_ramlog_prev_raw; each boot stamps a boot
	  counter, the slot and a build id into page A.
